AWS Lambda
AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service.
AWS Lambda has a maximum execution time of 15 minutes per invocation.
S3 bucket = Region-specific, but bucket name = globally unique

It allows you to run your application code without creating or managing an EC2 server.
AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service that lets you run your code without creating or managing EC2 servers.
Interview answer

AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service used to run code in response to events without managing servers. 
It is useful for event-driven tasks such as API processing, S3 file processing, SQS message processing, scheduled jobs,
and backend operations. We generally pay based on the number of requests and the time our functions execute.
Think of it like:

User
  ↓
API Gateway
  ↓
AWS Lambda
  ↓
DynamoDB / RDS
Why use Lambda?

With EC2:

You create EC2
     ↓
Install OS/software
     ↓
Run your application
     ↓
Manage server
     ↓
Pay for running server

With Lambda:

Event happens
     ↓
Lambda runs your code
     ↓
Code finishes
     ↓
Lambda stops

AWS manages the servers for you.

Simple example

Suppose a user uploads an image to S3:

User
 ↓
S3 bucket
 ↓
Lambda triggered
 ↓
Resize image
 ↓
Save resized image

You don't need to keep an EC2 server running just to wait for uploads.

Interview answer

AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service that runs code in response to events without requiring us to provision or manage servers such as EC2. We pay based on the compute time and requests used.

Important: Lambda doesn't mean there are no servers. AWS manages the servers for you.


AWS Lambda is paid, but you generally pay only when your Lambda function is used.

Example:

User request
     ↓
Lambda runs for 2 seconds
     ↓
Function finishes
     ↓
You are charged for the request + execution time
Lambda vs EC2
	EC2	Lambda
Server	You manage it	AWS manages it
Payment	Usually while instance is running	Based mainly on requests + execution time
Scaling	You configure it	AWS handles scaling
Server maintenance	Yes	No

Simple interview line:

"Lambda follows a serverless, pay-per-use model. We are charged based on the number of requests and the compute duration of the function."

There is also an AWS Lambda free tier, subject to AWS's current pricing terms.



Why do we use Lambda?

The main reason is:

We don't want to continuously run a server for a small piece of work.

Example without Lambda

Suppose your application needs to send a welcome email whenever a new user registers.

You could use:

User
 ↓
EC2 Server
 ↓
Application
 ↓
Send Email

But the EC2 server needs to keep running even when nobody registers.

10:00 AM → No user
11:00 AM → No user
12:00 PM → No user
01:00 PM → New user → Send email

You are keeping the server available all the time.

With Lambda
New User Registers
        ↓
      Lambda
        ↓
 Send Welcome Email
        ↓
      Finish

Lambda runs only when needed.

That's one of the biggest reasons we use Lambda.

Lambda is event-driven

Lambda usually runs because of an event.

For example:

S3 upload
   ↓
Lambda

API request
   ↓
Lambda

DynamoDB change
   ↓
Lambda

Scheduled time
   ↓
Lambda

SQS message
   ↓
Lambda

So Lambda is commonly used for event-driven applications.


